{"format":"broccoli-bootstrap-plan/1","id":"planning-review-mvp","title":"Persistent planning and review MVP","createdOn":"2026-09-27","status":"implementation","authority":{"phase":"temporary-bootstrap","canonicalFile":"plan.json","readableProjection":"PLAN.md","rule":"This is a temporary planning record, not implemented architecture or runtime data. PLAN.md is generated from plan.json. After verified import, the application becomes the only planning authority; replace these temporary planning details with a receipt pointing to app IDs.","importerContract":"This bootstrap format is a one-time intake format, not the final application schema. Import must use the application mutation service and validate every record; never copy this file directly over app storage. Fold bootstrap goal, scope and successCriteria into the task description without losing their wording; these planning-file fields do not prescribe structured application fields. Before retiring bootstrap files, import the complete approved specification (all global contracts, boundaries, operation guarantees, component composition, screenshot corrections, decisions and review records), not only task records. Preserve an immutable source snapshot and referenced visual assets as app-owned evidence with verified hashes. Fold actionable requirements into the corresponding live task descriptions/relations; later amendments occur only through the app. The source snapshot is historical evidence, never a second editable planning authority."},"objective":"Use the app itself to plan, persist, inspect and review this MVP and subsequent work, while Codex can perform the same CRUD operations through local tools. Deliver the independent-column Tasks board and the nested task graph timeline with a complete contextual CRUD editor/review surface.","boundaries":["One existing buildspace is enough for the first usable release. Persist its stable context ID; do not decide workspace/system ownership boundaries for all future task graphs.","All activities are tasks. Purpose comes from scope, description and goals; there is no separate Activity or Epic entity and no mandatory planning/investigation/development sequence.","One canonical task model and contributor-owned operation contracts supply both views and MCP. The UI invokes the same relational operations exposed to agents; neither a standalone planning API nor a second task store may become a shortcut.","The first version is a manually operated planning/review application. Moving into Queued records placement and agreed scope; it does not start an agent. Existing explicit execution entry points remain separate.","Reuse existing session, snapshot, check and review capabilities where they fit. Integrate the specifically requested relational-engine/Harness/MCP path through explicit package contracts. This does not reinstate unrelated earlier Systems lifecycle bookkeeping.","Mockups are imperfect layout references. Written corrections are authoritative. No duration/cost fields, placeholders, measurement, aggregate panels or instrumentation work in the MVP. Keep graph structure and event chronology without a calibrated time scale.","Node is the generic reflected view; Review and Graph are specialized presentations of the same underlying nodes. Use existing typed fields and relations for the MVP view contract; do not introduce a speculative ECS framework or duplicate node models.","A gray queue range and range endpoints are schematic lifecycle presentation; they do not reintroduce excluded numeric duration/cost measurement. Comments/check timestamps remain provenance.","Written final requirements and exact edge topology override image glyph artifacts. Design is accepted; runtime behavior, source verification and operation parity still require implementation evidence."],"references":[{"label":"Tasks board layout","path":"C:/Users/vince/.codex/generated_images/01a0c5f4-7593-7072-8985-289e55a9faf9/exec-2fcdc9f8-f09e-467c-b521-adf8ec18909f.png","qualification":"Accepted layout with one correction: remove New priority outside an active drag. MVP has no dragging, so never show that overlay."},{"label":"Task graph timeline layout","path":"C:/Users/vince/.codex/generated_images/01a0c5f4-7593-7072-8985-289e55a9faf9/exec-58525cfa-508d-4e26-93bf-0122fc9b5db5.png","qualification":"Accepted conceptual direction, superseded visually by current V3/V4: no arrowheads, no child counts, plain range labels, approximate one-third graph and two-thirds inspector, no duration breakdown."},{"label":"Active repository guidance","path":"C:/Users/vince/Documents/GitHub/Btr/TheBroccoli/AGENTS.md"},{"label":"Verification requirements","path":"C:/Users/vince/Documents/GitHub/Btr/TheBroccoli/TESTING.md"},{"label":"UI antipatterns","path":"C:/Users/vince/Documents/GitHub/Btr/TheBroccoli/.agents/skills/ui-antipatterns/SKILL.md"},{"label":"Broccoli Local plugin manifest","path":"C:/Users/vince/.codex/plugins/cache/personal/broccoli-local/0.1.0/.codex-plugin/plugin.json"},{"label":"MCP contributor","path":"C:/Users/vince/Documents/GitHub/Btr/TheBroccoli/Systems/Depth2/Mcp/src/index.ts"},{"label":"Agent operation discovery","path":"C:/Users/vince/Documents/GitHub/Btr/TheBroccoli/Systems/Depth1/Harness/src/index.ts"},{"label":"Correct graph wiring reference","path":"C:/Users/vince/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/21/01a0c5f4-7593-7072-8985-289e55a9faf9/mvp-plan/images/graph-correct-connectors.png","qualification":"Exact edge topology is confirmed by user; sidebar hierarchy is obsolete. Final written topology is authoritative."},{"label":"Software implementation design","path":"C:/Users/vince/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/21/01a0c5f4-7593-7072-8985-289e55a9faf9/mvp-plan/SOFTWARE-DESIGN.md"}],"observedStartingPoint":[{"source":"app/workflow.mjs","fact":"Persists inputs and has task updates, prerequisites and queue execution. Inputs currently have a single taskId; queueing requires a model and can call tick(). Replace these assumptions for planning CRUD, rather than reuse queue() as a board move."},{"source":"app/sessions.mjs","fact":"Has canonical harness task records, events, sessions, metrics, snapshots and atomic per-task writes. create() currently inspects a workspace; a title-only planning task must not require a session or a Git checkout scan."},{"source":"app/checkpoints.mjs","fact":"Supports submitted snapshots, required reviewers, typed comments and author-controlled closing. Review iterations currently chain linearly, and freeze logic locks overlapping checkout roots."},{"source":"app/server.mjs","fact":"The compact app has authenticated HTTP routes and server-sent updates, while the configured relational host/MCP path is a separate composition. T01 must resolve how the active app shares the contributor owner; do not assume these are already one runtime or duplicate persistence between them."},{"source":"app/public/timeline.js and app/public/review-model.js","fact":"Current timeline is a checkpoint list with automatic sample data, not the new nested task graph. Production planning views must not silently fall back to samples."},{"source":"app/browser-components.json and app/public/layout.js","fact":"Splitter, tree, schema-form and view-toolbar components already have explicit imports. No task-duration graph component is registered in this manifest."},{"source":"Repository worktree","fact":"Many pre-existing modified and untracked files are present. Establish an implementation baseline without reverting them or claiming them as new work."},{"source":"Broccoli Local plugin and relational-host.json","fact":"Cached plugin targets http://127.0.0.1:43120/mcp. Host configuration enables Harness and MCP contributors. A read-only authenticated tools/list probe did not reach the endpoint in this session; no Broccoli MCP tools are currently exposed in this conversation. This is not proof the implementation is absent."},{"source":"Systems/Depth2/Mcp/src/index.ts","fact":"MCP tools/list discovers contributor-owned Agent usage records; tools/call invokes the owning relational operation. Existing source tests cover discovery, invocation, ownership, authentication and errors; those tests were inspected, not run this turn."},{"source":"Systems/Depth1/Harness/src/index.ts","fact":"Agent usage carries tool name, operation identity, input schema and read/write effect. Discovery validates operation ownership and schema alignment. Extend this path rather than duplicating tool schemas in a new CLI."},{"source":"app/checkpoints.mjs:71-135 (read during planning review, not a runtime test)","fact":"Existing owner rejects new submissions and reply/close/reopen actions once checkpoint status is passed; comment creation also requires status review. Retain this terminal-review behavior for MVP rather than introducing retroactive passage withdrawal."},{"source":"app/public/timeline.js:277-328 and app/checkpoints.mjs:136-179 (read during planning review)","fact":"Existing UI provides human approval/revise actions and owner checks reviewer identity/order. These are reuse candidates for the early R02 human path, not proof of secure end-to-end principal separation. T01/T02 must validate/harden the transport-to-owner actor binding; never accept an agent-supplied human label."},{"source":"app/checkpoints.mjs:frozen and act(finish), inspected for fresh plan review on 2026-09-27","fact":"Existing freeze is held while checkpoint status is review. Finish/revise returns to working only after all required reviewers have outcomes. Threads persist across iterations; root thread author owns closure, and replies have no independent close gate. These observations justify preserving finish/closure boundaries while changing new iteration identity; no runtime tests were performed."}],"minimumContract":[{"name":"Task identity and definition","text":"Stable internal node/task ID and context ID, title, one optional freeform description, typed fields and explicit relations. A title alone is valid. Scope/goals/success criteria remain prose in the description, not separate authored fields. Creator is an immutable actor relation derived by the owner, not a separately editable property. The same task shape supports nesting and optional descriptive purpose; a purpose is not a separate Activity/Epic entity. Use existing readable node references and navigation; no new URL UI component is required. Persisted references obey Persisted graph links."},{"name":"Containment versus prerequisites","text":"parentId means containment; dependsOn task IDs mean prerequisites. Both are explicit and independently validated for missing references and cycles. A task cannot require an ancestor whose completion itself requires that task. Shared prerequisites remain one task, never duplicated to make a tree. External prerequisites are visible as references when outside the current nested graph."},{"name":"Placement and lifecycle","text":"Persist backlog, queued, running, blocked, completed and abandoned separately from purpose and review outcome. The board shows the first four task placements plus Inputs. Completed/abandoned work remains accessible in graph/history. Transitions are reversible with an event and a reason where needed; re-entry must not erase previous attempts. No automatic execution on CRUD, import or restart."},{"name":"Priority brackets","text":"Stable priority-group IDs plus an ordered list; display indices derive as0..n with0 highest. Creating a bracket atomically assigns at least one selected task; the UI collects bracket position and task selection before commit. There is no persisted create-empty-bracket operation. A bracket disappears when its final member leaves, and indices compact. Task moves/bracket insertion/reordering/removal validate atomically. Members have equal priority; display order promises no scheduler order."},{"name":"Inputs","text":"Inputs retain ID, title, reported classification, description/source/evidence and many-to-many links to tasks. A reported issue is not a verified bug. Linking or creating an investigation task does not endorse the report. Retain the original input after consolidation; changing input classification requires an explicit recorded edit. Review comments are input nodes related to their task/review; reply/reference relations can form a discussion graph instead of a mandatory separate thread entity. Reuse existing author/reply data through the canonical owner, never duplicate comment records. Task-associated discussion inputs are filtered out of the top-level Inputs column rather than being a different entity type. Exact thread-to-input adaptation belongs to T01; preserve author-owned closure semantics."},{"name":"Revision and history","text":"The service returns a graph revision. Mutations require expectedRevision and a request ID; stale writes return a conflict and retries cannot create duplicates. Definition changes, moves, dependency changes, queue episodes, attempt start/end and review actions create durable actor-attributed events. Changed plans retain their prior revisions; this is product history, not a parallel legacy schema."},{"name":"Execution attempts and observations","text":"Tasks have durable lifecycle events, queue episodes, outcomes and evidence references. Session attempts are subordinate observations only; post-review iterations have their own task IDs. Persist real order/available timestamps for audit, without measured duration/cost fields or axes. Render known queue episodes as gray schematic ranges, work/review/integration ranges with both endpoints, and actual human/agent involvement only. Unknown participation is absent, not an invented pair of icons. One session may be linked to several tasks via distinct turn ranges. Planned, queued, active, completed and abandoned work must be distinguishable from real data."},{"name":"Agreed work","text":"Record the scope/definition revision agreed for an activation. Moving a task to Queued does not authorize a broader goal or launch work. Explicit start can use an existing session or a manual external-work record. Pausing a parent does not silently change parallel branches; record the explicitly selected affected tasks."},{"name":"Review tasks","text":"A review is an ordinary task node presented through a specialized Review view. Its relations identify the exact reviewed task/attempt/definition or file snapshot revision. Review requirements are checks: automated execution, deterministic predicates, semantic reviewer decisions and discussion closure. Reuse existing owners/results and project them into one check collection; do not build a general policy engine or duplicate review state. At least one reviewer is required; required actors/order and mandatory checks are fixed on submission. Presented work stays frozen while reviews proceed. Decisions include approval, changes requested, blocked and abandonment. A blocked result blocks dependent work; independent branches pause only by explicit choice. For this MVP the required reviewer is the human; agent-review orchestration and automated semantic-review execution are deferred. Unified check presentation does not imply one Requires check edge for every source: semantic relations identify reviewed work, inputs and actors; repository checks are discovered at runtime. Persist submitted requirements and run provenance so later discovery changes cannot silently rewrite an existing review. Every new review in an iteration loop has a distinct task identity. Author readiness and reviewer outcome are different state transitions. Keep temporal executed-run evidence separate from submitted requirements; grouping in a tree is presentation of the same owners, not duplicate policy."},{"name":"Review comments and success","text":"Typed feedback is stored in authored input aggregates and replies as specified in Comments as grouped feedback. Each required discussion-closure check derives from those input states; it cannot be independently toggled to pass. All comments close before passage regardless of type, only their author closes/reopens them, and required human approval cannot be supplied by an agent. Required checks normally need passed evidence applicable to the submitted revision. Stale evidence remains visibly stale and blocks acceptance unless the human explicitly accepts that earlier passed result for this target revision, with a recorded reason and exact source run. This never rewrites the run as current/passed-on-new-code, never authorizes an agent to waive freshness, and is not a general override for failed or never-run required checks. Informational results do not become mandatory by merely being linked. A planning-only review uses definition/result evidence without requiring a fake Git diff. Passed reviews are immutable: comments cannot be added/replied/reopened there, preserving the existing owner behavior. Later concerns create a fresh review task linked to the prior result and relevant new work; never silently revoke historical passage or alter completed attempts. Author reopen is supported only before passage. Any future retroactive revalidation policy is deferred."},{"name":"Data ownership","text":"A contributor-owned domain service is the single authority for planning tasks and relations; adapt/rework the existing task owner behind it instead of introducing a separate planning-task copy. Separate execution/session mechanics from title-only planning. T01 chooses the smallest explicit integration with the current app and configured relational host, including a single transaction/revision boundary. No general workflow engine or compatibility layer."},{"name":"Scope and actors","text":"Every command is scoped to an existing planning context and a server-established actor. MCP agent credentials cannot claim the human identity or close human comments. Reference IDs are validated within that context; repository attachments remain references and do not hard-code a global task-graph ownership model."},{"name":"UI/tool operation parity","text":"Every planning operation exposed to agents has a complete user-operable UI path through reusable components: task/input CRUD, containment, prerequisites, source links, priorities, state transitions, results/evidence, review actions and import/export. Authority-specific outcomes remain actor-specific: parity does not let agents impersonate a human or users close another author's thread."},{"name":"View selection","text":"A shared view descriptor declares required data/relations and supported operations. A single adapter matches those against existing types, typed fields, relations and actor permissions; specialized views do not hard-code their own task-type switches. Node is always available for readable nodes; missing write operations yield a read-only presentation. Default is Review for nodes with a review contract, otherwise Node; Graph remains available for applicable task structure. Explicit valid URL/view selection wins over defaults. Missing permissions or data disable/omit affected actions, not the readable node. This bounded adapter can later use components without replacing each view. No ECS storage migration or plugin framework in MVP."},{"name":"Persisted graph links","text":"Persist graph references through the existing decentralized graph serialization format and its identity/resolution contracts; inspect and reuse them rather than introducing a competing URL/locator encoding. After serialize, discard in-memory objects, deserialize and reconnect the graph, links must identify the same intended nodes and revisions/evidence, with shared reference identity preserved. This includes cross-repository/context references within authorized scope, containment/prerequisites, review targets, discussion/reply anchors and successor links. Runtime origin/port is not the identity of a persisted graph link. Test restart and changed runtime binding; do not assume round-trip validity merely because the format exists. Missing/deleted/unavailable targets remain explicit unresolved references with identity retained, never silent substitution. User-facing URL component, copy/open redesign, browser-entry guarantees after a port change, local DNS/proxy/protocol integration and new presentation-state URL codecs are deferred. Existing navigation can be reused; this MVP does not require a new URL presentation feature."},{"name":"Revisions and comparison","text":"Node toolbar selects a target revision and explicit comparison base with the existing no-diff / inline-diff / side-by-side-diff segmented icon control. Compare typed field values and connected relations between immutable graph revisions scoped to the selected node; baseline defaults to the preceding revision and stays explicit. No prior revision means no comparison and a contextual reason. Historical versions are read-only; edits require returning to live. No new text-diff engine: reuse existing diff controls/rendering where suitable, add a shared structured comparison projection where needed. Relation add/remove/change and text fields must both be represented. Selected revisions/base/mode are addressable; server live expectedRevision remains the mutation concurrency token, not the displayed historical revision. Expose only one revision selector in the toolbar; comparison-base selection belongs inside that control when diff is enabled, not a second permanently visible dropdown."},{"name":"Repository checks MVP","text":"Detect checks from existing repository-owned conventions/configuration and aggregate them for the selected change/review scope across repositories. One owning run-checks operation exposed through MCP and UI takes bounded granularity (relevant changes by default, repository/check subset, or explicitly requested broader suite). The runner resolves applicable checks; the model need not inspect a list and assemble commands. A read/discovery operation is compatible but not a prerequisite to running. Reuse existing commands, selectors and report artifacts; record which checks were selected, run, failed, skipped, unsupported or not run, their source definition revision, execution revision/worktree fingerprint and timestamp. A shared check does not need a persisted graph edge simply to be discovered. MVP must not invent a generic change-impact algorithm: use repository-owned selection where available and make missing selection support explicit."},{"name":"Check applicability and evidence","text":"Applicability belongs to the check/provider predicate rather than universal system inference. Shared predicate infrastructure and user escape hatches for convention conflicts are follow-up work. For MVP reuse predicates/selectors the repository already supplies. Required change-relevant tests and required human review are the initial policy; full suite and performance tests are optional unless explicitly required for a specific review. Existing repository coverage reports can be attached/read with measured scope, effective thresholds and provenance where available. Do not invent a coverage threshold algorithm or missing data; threshold computation/overrides and file/function-level policy tooling belong to the follow-up. Timestamp and checked-version identity remain available despite omission of task duration/cost metrics. Latest scope clarification: result and threshold are sufficient for initial coverage UI; do not implement line-by-line source-code coverage or detailed exploration in MVP. Delivery exception explicitly authorized by the latest user instruction: SD01/R02 and independent implementation verification use independently assigned agents; actual final acceptance is R03 by the human. This is checkpoint policy, not agent orchestration or human impersonation."},{"name":"Shared checks follow-up","text":"B01 is a separate non-MVP backlog task for reusable version-pinned check/pipeline infrastructure usable by Broccoli and templates without duplicating the test system. Code/API-defined checks and repository-linked versioned definitions are the design direction; no YAML requirement or premature selection of Git submodules. Applicability predicates, explicit forcing when conventions conflict, tools-first semantic capabilities and detailed coverage threshold/scoping behavior are included in needs analysis. No Codex-specific hook dependency. Bounded discovery of reusable seams during implementation is allowed; implementation of this backlog scope is not silently added to MVP."},{"name":"Iteration identity and graph relations","text":"Each work iteration following a review is a NEW task, and the next review is a NEW task. Explicit continues-task, responds-to-review and reviews-target relations connect them to the earlier task and exact reviewed version; containment remains separate. Existing low-level session attempts may remain subordinate evidence, but must never stand in for the distinct work/review task identities. Create continuation work atomically with its lineage relations via an explicit idempotent operation. Later create a separate draft review and its reviews-target/continuation relations via another idempotent operation. Do not fabricate future target evidence at work creation; exact review target is fixed only on explicit submission. Neither operation starts execution. A downward return edge is layout of that relation, not a reverse prerequisite or a model cycle. Preserve readable relations and occurrence ordering when folded geometry is not globally chronological."},{"name":"Review preparation and submission","text":"Review task starts as draft. Author identity is an immutable actor relation, shown in Author with preparation/readiness controls. The authorized author may attach the target, configure requirements and add explanatory comments before explicit Submit for review. Submission atomically validates at least one reviewer, fixes the exact target and policy revision, records ready/submitted state and activates the reviewed-scope freeze. Draft does not pass or freeze implementation; submitted review permits discussion/check evidence and actor decisions but no implementation changes in that scope. Rerunning checks is permitted observation, not permission to alter submitted source. Source-mutating commands must not silently run under the freeze. Generic Node updates cannot change a submitted target/policy or forge readiness/approval."},{"name":"Comments as grouped feedback","text":"A submitted comment/reply is an authored input node with an ordered flat list of stable feedback rows (type and freeform text); Fix, Explain, Suggestion and Propose are supported. Several rows may discuss one anchored subject. Reply/reference edges relate inputs; do not create a separate authoritative thread store. One reusable component handles new comments, posted comments and replies; rows have type/text and author-permitted trash controls, aggregate boundary and footer actions. Draft removal is local; persisted edits/removals are attributable amendments and do not silently mark the comment closed. Even when all rows are removed, an open persisted comment stays author-closeable and blocking until explicitly closed; removal cannot evade review gates or destroy audit evidence. Submit rejects an empty new comment. Close/reopen is owned by the author of the root comment/input aggregate and resolves that discussion, as in the existing checkpoint owner. Replies remain authored input nodes for attribution/editing, but do not add independent closure gates. A new top-level comment creates its own closure obligation; replies do not silently reopen a closed discussion. Explicit root-author reopen is required before further replies. Another actor cannot remove the author's feedback or close their discussion. Passed-review discussion data is sealed. Anchor stores target revision, repo/path/side/line range or node/revision/field context; revision switching must never silently re-anchor a comment."},{"name":"Review target and changes","text":"Code review submission captures an immutable multi-repository target manifest, including stable repo identity, exact base/target commits where available and captured content for uncommitted changes. A moving branch name or current worktree alone is not an exact reviewed version. Reuse existing snapshots/content storage; an aggregation node may reference it, without requiring a new general snapshot framework. Changes opens the selected review/iteration target and shows real before/after source plus anchored feedback. File-tree control switches All files/Modified files; text/diff modes use existing renderers. Iteration selection above the tree supports one or more iterations and a combined diff; graph/timeline navigation to a specific iteration sets the same selection. Combine the selected changes per repository, not just the first base to last target if that would silently include unselected work. Reuse composition where possible; conflicting/noncomposable selections report a precise conflict rather than inventing a combined result. A multi-selection cannot pretend to be one submitted target; posting requires a named exact target/anchor."},{"name":"Check tree and execution controls","text":"Under Author, Checks contains Reviewers first and Runnables second. Each has Mandatory and Optional groups. Mandatory Reviewers includes required human decisions and the derived Comments closed requirement; optional reviewers never waive that universal closure gate. Agent-review orchestration remains deferred. Runnables uses a single-column nested tree; children expose latest results for the selected iteration, repository/subcheck granularity, coverage result/threshold, run identity and revision. Selection uses normal click/Ctrl/Shift/Ctrl+Shift highlights, no checkboxes. Runnables toolbar order is play all, play mandatory, play selected, stop; only these Runnables row controls run checks, no per-item play or bottom run button. All/mandatory/selected resolve to executable leaf checks within this review scope, deduplicate ancestor/child selection and use the same granular run-checks operation. Selected result rows open evidence and are not executable. Empty selection disables selected-run. Stop targets active run groups in this review context, returns affected IDs, requests cancellation, retains partial logs/results and never records cancellation as pass; unsupported cancellation is explicit. Run controls never execute human reviewer/closure rows. Runnables is a regular expandable row nested under Checks, at the same level as Reviewers, not a separate panel heading. Keep its four actions right-aligned within that row. Mandatory/Optional are its child rows. The view tools toolbar spans the full right pane above its inner splitter; far-right tools belong to that same toolbar. Delivery exception explicitly authorized by the latest user instruction: SD01/R02 and independent implementation verification use independently assigned agents; actual final acceptance is R03 by the human. This is checkpoint policy, not agent orchestration or human impersonation."},{"name":"Review progression, freeze and successor gates","text":"Draft has no review freeze. Submitted/pending review holds its scope freeze while required reviewers inspect. A required blocked/pending-decision outcome retains the freeze and blocks dependants until that actor explicitly resolves it. Approve passes only when all mandatory requirements, required actors and discussion closure allow; passing seals the target, outcomes and discussions and releases that review's freeze. Changes-requested completes a non-passing round after all required reviewers have finished; it releases that review's freeze so explicit correction work can begin, while dependent gates remain unsatisfied. Abandon is an explicit terminal non-passing review/branch disposition, releases its freeze and does not enable dependants. Releasing one review never releases another active overlapping review's freeze. Independent branches are not paused without an explicit action; shared-checkout freeze limitations remain visible. Earlier changes-requested outcome/target never changes to passed. New work uses responds-to-review, not a prerequisite that would require the rejected review to pass. A later replacement draft review may explicitly supersede that old review: one validated transaction records the successor relation and replaces an enumerated set of still-pending review-prerequisite edges from the old review to the new review. Retain edge-change/actor/reason history. Only listed dependants change; completed dependants and unrelated gates do not. The successor must cover the same acceptance obligation/lineage and preserve mandatory reviewers/checks and unresolved discussion references; scope/policy weakening needs explicit human re-planning, not a generic unlink. Generic mutations cannot remove a protected review gate to bypass it. The new draft/submitted review is itself unsatisfied until it passes. No pass propagation merely by following lineage. Carry open root discussion inputs by reference into the successor and make their original anchors inspectable; do not clone or silently close them. The new review cannot pass until their authors close them. Non-passed-round discussion replies/closure may continue; passage seals the included discussion set."}],"surfaces":[{"name":"Tasks board","requirements":["Independent columns in this exact order: Inputs, Backlog, Queued, Running, Blocked. No shared rows across columns and no view/tools strip above them.","Inputs is flat. Backlog shows only tasks with no parent. Queued groups tasks into numbered priority brackets. Running and Blocked are flat lists. Nested tasks with active placements remain reachable; their parent is available in details.","Reuse the shell, breadcrumb and adjustable left/right split. The right pane is blank without a selection. Selecting an input/task opens the minimal persisted editor; task navigation opens its graph.","Use ordinary labelled controls/context actions for create, edit, move, delete and priority changes. No drag-and-drop, drag insertion overlay or speculative toolbar in this release.","Task rows expose enough identity to distinguish them; completion/history access must not silently hide work. Creating a child from the selected parent makes it reachable through that parent graph.","Compose a board container with independently scrolling reusable columns unless the existing table already provides the same independent-content semantics cleanly. Audit header rendering, sorting/filter/action contracts and sizing before extracting or extending shared components. Reuse table-header controls where applicable; do not force common rows or table semantics on unrelated lists. Preserve per-column scroll and selection across updates."]},{"name":"Nested task graph timeline","requirements":["Breadcrumb navigates Tasks -> chosen task -> nested task. Parent containment defines graph scope; explicit prerequisite edges must not be inferred from timestamps or containment. Shared/external prerequisites are reachable references, not duplicated tasks.","Present the model faithfully from the first usable view: parallel paths, shared prerequisites, integration tasks, stopped branches and nesting. Collapsed children use > without child counts. No arrowheads; selected relation details provide labelled predecessor/successor identity and relationship meaning.","MVP graph uses schematic, unscaled task/attempt ranges and event dots. Recorded chronology is newest at top, and planned work has distinct styling/region; range length does not claim measured duration. Concurrent tasks and dependency edges must remain faithful even where vertical position alone cannot express both chronology and dependency. Do not place planned work below a false Now boundary or show unfinished work as completed. Omit a Now line unless its meaning is actually supported.","Purpose fixes range color: planning lavender, execution teal, review sage, integration tan/orange, queue gray; unknown purpose neutral. Outcomes use end-dot icon/color only. All labels are plain/unboxed with one representation. Participant icons/counts remain when known; no invented involvement.","No duration, cost, elapsed-time axis, telemetry placeholder or category breakdown anywhere in the MVP. Existing audit timestamps need not be removed, but measuring/aggregating/presenting durations or costs is excluded.","Selecting a task or attempt opens Node or another supported selected view. Evidence links reuse existing contextual session/files/check viewers. Start with one-third graph and two-thirds right panel. No graph drawing, zoom machinery or full session drill-down required.","Each new post-review work and review is a distinct task. Fold a failed review's UPPER end dot rightward and DOWN to the next iteration's LOWER start dot; place work/review pairs laterally. Do not divide graph into Work and Review columns. Keep independent work/review branches possible. Folded ordering is not a global elapsed-time axis; provide accessible relationship/sequence details.","Every recorded range has visible start/end dots, queue episodes are gray, review results change only the end dot, and participation reflects actual actors/counts. Abandoned branch end has no outgoing success edge. Successful branches may converge into explicit integration (through its queue episode where present).","Golden topology for acceptance: scope forks comparison/prototype/spec; each has its review. Prototype review ends abandoned. Comparison/spec successes merge to integration queue/start. Integration ends in failed review; only that result links down to distinct correction task start, followed by distinct review. There is no edge from correction start to spec/prototype and no outgoing edge above prototype abandonment."]},{"name":"Shared contextual CRUD and review views","requirements":["Blank when unselected. Selected input/task uses shared reusable record forms, relationship controls, commands, lists and evidence/review views. Editing, creation and deletion must all be possible without agent assistance.","Task controls cover title and freeform description, create child, change parent, add/remove prerequisites and input links, state changes, priority group insertion/reordering and safe deletion with reference handling.","Input controls cover create/read/edit/delete, reported classification, evidence and links to one or more tasks. Creating/linking investigative work does not silently confirm an issue report.","Provide result/evidence/attempt controls, review creation/submission, reviewer configuration, typed comments/replies, author close/reopen and permitted review outcomes. Show conflicts, blockers and errors through shared components.","Provide accessible UI entry points for bootstrap import dry-run/apply and plan export, using the same operations as tools; a raw JSON file may be an input to import, but routine CRUD must not require hand-editing JSON.","No top-level Files/Checks/Preview strip in Tasks. Reuse those viewers contextually. No speculative board toolbar or drag insertion overlay at rest.","Generic view is named Node, generated from declared field types and connected relations; creator appears as a read-only relation in the same flat list as parent/prerequisites/evidence. One freeform description. Persisted links use the existing graph serialization contracts; URL UI is deferred. No separate State field; placement commands remain discoverable through shared command actions.","Use a shared top view-toolbar with view selector, revision/base selection, three icon comparison modes and directly exposed applicable actions. Do not add duplicate Open graph navigation or overflow nesting when actions fit. Node history access stays in the toolbar; Review has no History entry.","Review toolbar contains local tabs Overview, Changes, Preview in this order, one revision control and applicable shared tools. Tabs retain normal top/side spacing with zero bottom gap. No URL strip, duplicate identity/status panel or global Run checks button. View/tab changes update only the view/tools area and retain graph/tree selection, scroll and drafts.","Overview main pane contains a headerless multi-row comment composer and unified newest-first activity with comments and actual check-run records. Top-level file context, diff preview and feedback fill the comment width; only replies indent. Actions are footer controls, no side action column. Author and Checks sidebar uses the hierarchy/behavior in Check tree and execution controls.","Changes uses existing repository tree + file/diff layout. Replace Changes-count header with All files/Modified files toggle. Reuse identical comment/reply components without duplicate preview. Include no/inline/side-by-side icons and a single comparison/revision control. Preview reuses the existing preview viewer/controls for applicable selected targets; unsupported historical preview must be explicit, never silently show live content as reviewed version.","Review outcome actions remain in reviewer decision controls. Configure requirements through Node before submission. Latest result links/readouts always nest beneath the associated check; execution events remain in the activity feed. No metadata status columns, Required labels or per-item play controls.","Final screenshot corrections: Runnables remains an ordinary Checks tree row with right-side tools, not a second panel/header. The Overview activity/sidebar splitter starts at the content area below the shared view toolbar. Toolbar borders/tools span both content subpanes; the far-right link icon is a view tool, not a detached sidebar control. These written corrections override the last generated image; no extra generation is requested."]}],"tools":{"transport":"Use the existing Broccoli Local plugin -> MCP contributor -> graph-discovered Agent usage -> contributor-owned relational operation path. The UI invokes the same owning operations through the relational UI transport. First verify/repair composition and live plugin discovery, then publish planning operations there. No CLI-only MVP substitute and no second planning API/domain implementation.","proposedInvocation":"Exact planning tool names and input schemas will be authored by the owning contributor and discovered through existing Agent usage contracts. No callable planning tools are claimed to exist yet. MCP discovery plus a real Codex read/write round trip is an acceptance requirement.","operations":["Read/list tasks and inputs, fetch graph and declared field/relation/operation metadata, read immutable revisions and node comparison projections, resolve persisted graph references using existing serialization/identity contracts, inspect evidence/check requirements and export the plan.","Create/update/delete tasks and inputs; add/remove parent, prerequisites and input-task links. Reject dangling references and cycles. Deletion with children/references requires an explicit removal/reparenting plan; never silently cascade.","Move tasks, insert a priority group with selected task(s) atomically, reorder groups, remove empty groups after moves and record agreed activation scope. No execution side effects.","Record task results and manual attempts; attach existing session ranges, diffs/snapshots, checks and external evidence without inventing telemetry.","Create drafts/configure targets and requirements, author-submit readiness, create a distinct continuation work task and later a separate draft review, create/edit/delete permitted feedback rows, comment/reply and author close/reopen through owning operations. Authenticated outcomes derive passage; generic edits cannot bypass gates.","Dry-run and apply one-time bootstrap import atomically; return stable source-to-app ID mapping and a receipt. Repeating the same import request is idempotent.","Run checks at requested granularity through one contributor-owned operation: change scope, repository/check subset or explicit broader suite. Read/discover and inspect results remain available. Preserve source revisions and execution scope; no agent freshness waiver.","Cancel/stop active check-run groups scoped to a review with recorded actor and affected run IDs. Same command/status is exposed in MCP and UI; never equate cancelled or partially completed execution to passed evidence.","Explicitly replace enumerated pending review gates with a replacement draft review while recording successor relations, actor/reason and edge history. Validate same acceptance obligation, required policy and unresolved comment carry-forward; never mark the old review passed or allow generic unlink to bypass a protected gate."],"safeguards":["One contributor-owned operation implementation serves both MCP and UI; validation, review rules, revision checks and attribution must be identical. Transport adapters own no domain rules.","Reuse verified local plugin authentication without exposing credentials. Review actor identity must be established by the host, not a caller-supplied human/agent label; extend the current contract if necessary.","Successful commands return committed IDs/revision, validation conflicts are structured and actionable, and notifications are emitted only after persistence succeeds.","Delete removes a live planning item, not the audit evidence that an authorized deletion occurred. Redacted or unavailable content must not be presented as complete."]},"deferred":["Automatic queue scheduling, agent provisioning, unattended execution and leaving Codex as the working harness.","Drag-and-drop, graph drawing/editing, elaborate filtering, full alternate-view catalogue.","Automatic input classification, deduplication or conversion of reports to confirmed issues/tasks.","Full multi-user access administration, distributed synchronization and arbitrary workflow/policy builders.","Repository-wide lifecycle graph replacement, a finalized workspace/system ownership boundary